PatchClosure-Bench :: deser_sofahessian_blacklist :: real-Docker gate reproduction (REAL-APP-HTTP)
=================================================================================================
Date: 2026-07-15
Product: SOFA Hessian (com.alipay.sofa:hessian) -- OPEN SOURCE github.com/sofastack/sofa-hessian
Class: Hessian deserialization denylist bypass -- CWE-184 Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs + CWE-502
CVE pair: CVE-2019-9212 (V1 = incomplete fix) -> CVE-2024-46983 (V2 = complete)

RUN MODE: real-app-http. The deserialization sink is exercised by an EXTERNAL HTTP request. A REAL small Java
HTTP service (com.pcb.HessianDeserService, JDK built-in com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer) boots per version
with the REAL, UNMODIFIED com.alipay.sofa:hessian:${SH_VER} jar on its classpath and exposes:
  POST /deser  -- body = raw Hessian2-serialized bytes; the service deserializes them through the jar's own
                  default class-name blacklist (ClassNameResolverBuilder.buildDefault() -> NameBlackListFilter
                  over the bundled security/serialize.blacklist) exactly as a SOFA-RPC service deserializes a
                  Hessian request body, then echoes ${PCB_FLAG} in the response IFF the name passed the filter.
  GET  /health -- readiness probe.
No sofa-hessian source is copied or modified; only a tiny benign canary JAR (com.caucho.naming.Qname) is added
to the service classpath so the ORIGINAL shadow-class name is a genuinely resolvable class. The real,
unmodified NameBlackListFilter runs on the class NAME.

Versions (pinned maven jars behind the HTTP service; the REAL jar's bundled security/serialize.blacklist is the sink):
  V0 = com.alipay.sofa:hessian:3.3.13  (pre-CVE-2019-9212; blacklist lacks the caucho/xpath entries)
  V1 = com.alipay.sofa:hessian:3.5.4   (CVE-2019-9212 patched; adds Qname + Xstring)   <- TARGET / incomplete
  V2 = com.alipay.sofa:hessian:3.5.5   (CVE-2024-46983 patched; broad prefixes incl. com.sun.) <- complete

Sink verification (REAL blacklist, decompiled from the jars):
  * ClassNameResolverBuilder.buildDefault() -> ClassNameResolver + InternalNameBlackListFilter which loads
    `security/serialize.blacklist` from the pinned jar.
  * SerializerFactory.getDeserializer(String type) calls classNameResolver.resolve(type) during
    Hessian2Input.readObject() -- i.e. the blacklist is applied on class resolution while decoding the body.
  * NameBlackListFilter.inBlackList(): className.startsWith(prefix) over blackPrefixList (CASE-SENSITIVE).
  * NameBlackListFilter.resolve() only THROWS "Class <name> is in blacklist." when its slf4j LOGGER is
    non-null, which requires sofa-common-tools (LoggerSpaceManager) + a logging backend present -- exactly how
    the framework ships in production. The env pulls sofa-common-tools 1.3.15 + logback 1.2.11 + slf4j-api
    1.7.36 so the guard ENFORCES faithfully. (Without them the guard silently no-ops -- confirmed empirically.)

Blacklist evolution (VERBATIM from the jars' security/serialize.blacklist):
  CVE-2019-9212 (V0 3.3.13 -> 3.4.0.., present in V1 3.5.4) ADDED:
      com.caucho.naming.Qname
      com.sun.org.apache.xpath.internal.objects.Xstring
      (lowercase typos vs the real classes QName / XString -- CWE-184 incomplete list)
  CVE-2024-46983 (V1 3.5.4 -> V2 3.5.5) REPLACED the narrow enumeration with broad package PREFIXES,
      relevant additions:  com.sun.   org.apache.xalan.   org.apache.xpath.   (+~150 more), retaining
      com.caucho.naming.Qname.  `com.sun.` finally prefix-matches ...objects.XString.

Benign shadow-class probes (NO gadget chain, NO exec -- the request body is a benign Hessian2 typed map naming
only the class + a 2-string map; a name that passes the filter merely makes the service echo the ${PCB_FLAG}):
  ORIGINAL (CVE-2019-9212 seed): com.caucho.naming.Qname   (verbatim the 9212 blacklist entry; on classpath)
  RESIDUAL (CVE-2024-46983)     : com.sun.org.apache.xpath.internal.objects.XString  (real XString gadget FQCN;
                                  already present in the JDK java.xml module -- name-based filter governs)

